## E. What the extension must add

E1. **It starts from what the delivered paper leaves open.** The open items are
in its Sec. 7, its Table 1 and my release review:
- **O1. The race outside linear models.** In the ViT only a step-0 rule was
  tested, not the race.
  - It misplaces the weight-decay transition 2.6x.
  - It fails in the AdamW arm.
  - The predictor, EMA and masking predictions did not transfer: four of four
    mask settings lower the count where no change was registered, and none of
    six interventions crosses the collapse line.
- **O2. Nonlinear and deep encoders.**
  - Under random masks a tanh encoder gains rank after the race.
  - A two-layer encoder is mispredicted from its initial features.
  - There is no theory for nonlinear encoders.
- **O3. Far from the collapsed solution.**
  - There is no post-capture stage and no noise floor; round-off re-seeds lost
    directions.
  - The analysis is valid only until the first directions reach order one.
  - The rank fall at high masking ratio held in only some profiles.
- **O4. Adaptive optimisers.** AdamW is at most partly supported in linear
  models and fails in the ViT, yet practical JEPAs train with AdamW.
- **O5. Assumptions and test status.**
  - The closed form needs Assumption 1 (aligned statistics).
  - The depth correction was made post hoc and re-tested only with
    identity-initialised factors.
  - One EMA sign comparison failed.
  - E1 is a development set.
- **O6. Design.** Table 1 states what the theory implies for designing or
  choosing anti-collapse heuristics, but no prescription was tested
  prospectively.

The paper says which of these it takes up, and argues that they are the ones
that most advance the understanding of when and why JEPAs collapse. It need not
take up all of them. Those it leaves remain in its limitations. A direction
counts only when it is carried through to a new theoretical or empirical result.
A pilot, a demonstration, a plan or an untested conjecture is not a result.

E2. **New theory, where it is claimed.**
- Each statement is precise, with its assumptions.
- Each proof is complete, in the appendix if needed. An approximation is
  labelled as one.
- The new theory reduces to the delivered linear race where that race's
  assumptions hold, or the paper says why it does not.
- For a nonlinear or deep encoder, the paper states what plays the role of the
  per-direction drive and decay, and in which regime that holds (for example
  scale of initialisation, width, training time).
- The paper gives a numerical check of at least one closed-form prediction
  against exact or simulated dynamics. I check one independently.

E3. **New empirical results.**
- A predictive claim is tested on predictions fixed before the measurement.
  The pre-registration is committed before the runs and is byte-identical at
  delivery. Any fitted constant is declared as fitted.
- Each comparison has at least 3 seeds per configuration, more where runs are
  cheap. Intervals are given with their method.
- Sweeps cross each predicted threshold on both sides.
- Rank metrics are defined, and the conclusions are checked for robustness to
  the rank threshold.
- Where rank stands for representation quality, a downstream measure (for
  example a linear probe) ties the two together.
- Baselines are included wherever they predict the same quantity:
  - the static threshold;
  - the delivered step-0 rule and the delivered race;
  - the closest prior theory's criterion (E5).
- Failures are shown and discussed, not dropped.
- Claims about realistic data or architectures are scoped to the datasets,
  architectures and optimisers actually measured. Transfer to a realistic
  setting has to be measured in that setting, not inferred from linear models.
  A headline claim of realistic transfer needs more than the delivered
  CIFAR-10 ViT, or an explicit scope statement.

E4. **Design implications, where they are claimed.**
- A heuristic choice or design that the theory prescribes is tested against the
  practice default and the relevant prior designs.
- The test uses matched compute, seeds, rank and downstream accuracy.
- A prescription that is not tested is labelled a hypothesis.

E5. **Prior work and novelty under the new cutoff (18 October 2026).** The
cutoff moved from 1 Sep 2026 (objective #1) to 18 Oct 2026. Work that the
delivered paper had to exclude is now prior work, and an ICLR 2027 referee will
read the paper against it.
- **arXiv 2610.02344v1**, submitted 2026-10-01. De Melo Costa, Ahn, Popineau,
  Rimmel and Doan, "Drive vs. Decay: On the Training Dynamics of
  Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures". Its arXiv comment says it was
  accepted at NeurIPS 2026, and its code is at github.com/jose-melo/drive-vs-decay.
  According to its abstract, it:
  - linearises the JEPA gradient flow at the trivial fixed point into a driving
    force γ and a decay σ;
  - forms a per-mode stability ratio μ_i = γ_i/σ_i, which under approximate
    spectral decoupling factorises into data-side and predictor-side terms;
  - counts the unstable modes as tracking the rank;
  - predicts a phase boundary that it confirms on more than 800 Tabular-JEPA
    configurations;
  - unifies predictor scaling, masking ratio and EMA as shifts of μ;
  - introduces ResidualPred, an identity-biased attention predictor that
    improves rank and accuracy in I-JEPA on CIFAR-10/100, STL-10 and ImageNet.

  This is the closest prior work to both the delivered paper and the extension.
  The paper must:
  1. cite it;
  2. state precisely what is new relative to it, and revise any novelty claim
     of the retained paper that it pre-empts;
  3. compare against its criterion as a baseline on the same runs, wherever
     both predict the same quantity (rank, collapse boundary, the effect of a
     heuristic);
  4. if it claims a predictor design, compare against ResidualPred or justify
     why not.

  Novelty is judged after an actual reading of this work, by `idea-repo` for
  the brief and by me for the review, not against its abstract alone.
  If its code is used as a baseline, the commit is the one dated on or before
  the cutoff (`3176215b2d16`, 2026-10-01, per the brief), pinned and recorded.
- Other work from 1 Sep to 17 Oct 2026 that the brief (5.2) identifies. I checked
  the arXiv metadata on 2026-10-17.
  - **arXiv 2609.35288** (v1 2026-09-28, now at v2), "λ-JEPA: Spectral
    anti-collapse regularization". It uses λ-balance between layer weights in a
    two-layer linear network. It bears on the balance identity (Cor. α=1) and on
    any deep-encoder result. The paper cites it and positions against it if it
    claims a balance or deep-linear result.
  - **arXiv 2610.07540** (v1 2026-10-06), on preserving unstable modes through
    inverse dynamics in JEPA world models. It is tangential: cite it if the
    paper discusses partial or task-relevant collapse.
  - **arXiv 2608.27395** LeVJEPA (2026-08-27, already allowed) and LeJEPA. These
    are the heuristic-free alternative that a design section (E4) should
    mention.
  - arXiv 2609.23881 (MotionJEPA) and 2609.09630 (JEPA Policy) are allowed but
    less central.
